const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is the best Buildertrend alternative for subcontractors?',
    answer:
      'BuildWorkPro. Buildertrend is excellent software for residential builders and remodelers who sell to homeowners, but its client portal, selections, and allowances workflows are built around that business. BuildWorkPro is built for trade subcontractors who bid to GCs and bill monthly against a schedule of values — with bidding, AIA-style pay applications, change orders, site logs, time tracking, and CRM at $79/month flat.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does Buildertrend cost compared to BuildWorkPro?',
    answer:
      'As of mid-2026, Buildertrend uses quote-based pricing tied to your construction volume; before that change its published plans started around $339–$499 per month. BuildWorkPro publishes one flat price: $79/month or $790/year with every feature and unlimited users included — roughly a tenth of Buildertrend’s historical entry tier. Verify current Buildertrend pricing with Buildertrend.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Buildertrend built for subcontractors?',
    answer:
      'Buildertrend is primarily designed for home builders and remodelers — companies whose client is a homeowner. Its standout features (client portal, selections, allowances, financing tools) serve that relationship. A “specialty contractor” in Buildertrend’s marketing is typically a homeowner-facing reno trade, not a sub billing a GC. A subcontractor working under GCs has a different workflow: estimate to a GC, win the contract, bill progress monthly with retainage, and document the field. BuildWorkPro is designed around that loop.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Buildertrend have a free trial?',
    answer:
      'No. Buildertrend is demo-led: you schedule a call or submit a contact form. There is no self-serve trial where you can evaluate the product on your own work first. BuildWorkPro is the opposite — a 14-day free trial with every feature and no credit card, so you can run a real bid and pay application before paying.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should I look for in a Buildertrend alternative?',
    answer:
      'Match the tool to who you bill. If your customer is a homeowner, you want a client portal, selections, and allowances — Buildertrend’s strengths, and the reason most “best Buildertrend alternative” roundups compare other builder platforms. If your customer is a GC, look for schedule-of-values billing with retainage, proposals written for GCs, change orders that roll into the next pay app, and published pricing you can evaluate without a sales call.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is JobTread a good Buildertrend alternative?',
    answer:
      'For home builders and GCs who want budget-centric job financials and a strong training community, JobTread is one of the names that comes up most often. It is still builder-first, starts at $199/month plus per-user fees, and has no free trial. If you are the sub billing against someone else’s contract, that is the workflow BuildWorkPro is built around, at $79/month flat.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I switch from Buildertrend to BuildWorkPro?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Export your contacts, leads, and job list, then use BuildWorkPro’s guided CSV import wizards to bring in contacts, leads, products, and projects. The 14-day free trial (no credit card) is enough time to run a real bid and pay application before deciding.',
  },
