---

<Layout
  title="Buildertrend Alternative 2026 for Subcontractors"
  description="Buildertrend alternative for subcontractors (2026): homeowner tools vs GC billing. Features, pricing, and who each fits. BuildWorkPro is $79/month flat."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-12">
      <div class="text-center">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Compare</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">
          Buildertrend Alternative 2026 for Subcontractors
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-slate-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Looking for a Buildertrend alternative? Buildertrend is built for home builders and
          remodelers whose client is a homeowner. If your client is a GC — and your month ends with
          a pay application, not a homeowner invoice — you're shopping in the wrong aisle.
          BuildWorkPro runs the subcontractor's workflow for
          <a href="/pricing/" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-brand-600"
            >$79/month flat</a
          >.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8">
          <CompareCta />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Verdict box (answer-first for skimmers and AI search) -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-16">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-brand-100 bg-brand-50 p-8">
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-3">The short version</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          <strong>Choose Buildertrend</strong> if you build or remodel homes and need homeowner-facing
          tools: a client portal, selections and allowances, and consumer financing. <strong
            >Choose BuildWorkPro</strong
          > if you're a specialty sub — electrical, plumbing, HVAC, roofing, concrete, framing — who bids
          to GCs, bills monthly against a schedule of values with retainage, and wants published flat
          pricing instead of a quote process.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Comparison table -->
    <section id="comparison" class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-8 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 text-center mb-10">
        BuildWorkPro vs Buildertrend at a glance
      </h2>
      <ComparisonTable rows={comparisonRows} competitor="Buildertrend" />
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-slate-400 px-2">
        *Buildertrend moved to quote-based pricing and no longer publishes plan prices; the range
        reflects its previously published entry tiers and independent reporting as of June 2026.
        Verify current pricing with Buildertrend. For last-published tiers, renewal quotes, and
        switching costs, see
        <a href="/blog/buildertrend-pricing/" class="underline hover:text-slate-600"
          >Buildertrend pricing and cost</a
        >.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <CompareCta secondaryHref="/pricing/" secondaryLabel="See pricing" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Who each fits -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
        <h2>Who each product is actually for</h2>
        <p>
          Most “best Buildertrend alternative” lists compare other home-builder platforms. That is
          the right shortlist if you sell to homeowners. It is the wrong shortlist if you are a
          trade sub whose customer is a general contractor. The split is who you bill — not how many
          modules the brochure lists.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Buildertrend fits</strong> custom home builders, remodelers, and homeowner-facing specialty
          trades. The product is organized around a client relationship: selections and allowances, a
          portal for the homeowner, consumer financing, and residential draws. If that is your business,
          stay in that category —
          <a href="/compare/jobtread-alternative/">JobTread</a> is the builder-first name that comes up
          most often next to Buildertrend.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>BuildWorkPro fits</strong> specialty subcontractors —
          <a href="/solutions/electrical-contractors/">electrical</a>, plumbing,
          <a href="/solutions/hvac-contractors/">HVAC</a>, roofing, concrete, framing,
          <a href="/solutions/glazing-contractors/">glazing</a> — who bid to GCs and get paid on progress.
          The loop is estimate → win → monthly
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/">pay application</a> with retainage → closeout. That is
          a different job than sending a homeowner an invoice, which is why a Buildertrend alternative
          for subs is not the same product as a Buildertrend alternative for builders.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are still mapping GC software vs sub software more broadly, start with
          <a href="/blog/subcontractor-vs-general-contractor-software/"
            >subcontractor vs general contractor software</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Feature-by-feature -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
        <h2>Features that actually differ</h2>
        <h3>Progress billing vs residential invoicing</h3>
        <p>
          Subs billing GCs live on a schedule of values, stored materials, and retainage — AIA-style
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/">pay applications</a> that match how the prime contract
          pays. That workflow is first-class in BuildWorkPro, including the
          <a href="/blog/aia-pay-application-guide/">AIA G702/G703-style pay app</a> path. Buildertrend
          is oriented to residential invoicing and draws for a homeowner client. Both are billing; they
          are not the same billing.
        </p>
        <h3>Bids written for a GC, not a kitchen selection</h3>
        <p>
          A sub’s proposal needs line items, labor rates, markup, and exclusions a GC can evaluate.
          BuildWorkPro’s
          <a href="/features/construction-bidding/">construction bidding</a> is built for that, then converts
          a won bid into the project you will bill. Buildertrend’s proposals are oriented to homeowner
          clients — a different audience and a different document.
        </p>
        <h3>Change orders that roll into the next pay app</h3>
        <p>
          Scope changes on a GC job have to be priced, approved, and billed against the updated
          contract value. BuildWorkPro
          <a href="/features/change-orders/">change orders</a> are numbered against the project and flow
          into the next pay application. Buildertrend also handles change orders — in the homeowner /
          builder context where selections and client approval are the point.
        </p>
        <h3>CRM: GC pipeline vs homeowner clients</h3>
        <p>
          Both products include a sales pipeline. The difference is who sits in it. BuildWorkPro’s
          <a href="/features/construction-crm/">construction CRM</a> is GC, owner, and vendor contacts
          with leads that convert to bids and jobs. Buildertrend’s CRM is organized around the homeowner
          relationship that the rest of the platform serves.
        </p>
        <h3>Field documentation</h3>
        <p>
          Daily logs and time tracking are included on both sides. BuildWorkPro’s
          <a href="/features/site-logs/">site logs</a> and
          <a href="/features/time-tracking/">time tracking</a> document self-performed work for the sub’s
          own record — not a homeowner progress feed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Honest section -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
        <h2>Where Buildertrend genuinely wins</h2>
        <p>
          For a custom home builder or remodeler, Buildertrend's homeowner-facing tools are the
          point: clients pick finishes through selections and allowances, follow progress in a
          portal, approve change orders, and even arrange financing. If that's your business,
          Buildertrend earns its price — and you should still walk into the quote call with a range.
          We assembled last-published plans, renewal quotes, and switching costs in
          <a href="/blog/buildertrend-pricing/">Buildertrend pricing and cost</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of that machinery helps a trade sub. Your GC doesn't want a selections portal — they
          want a clean bid with labor rates and exclusions, a monthly pay application that matches
          the schedule of values, and change orders priced and approved before the work happens.
          That billing loop — bid → contract → monthly pay app with retainage → closeout — is the
          core of BuildWorkPro, not an adaptation of a homeowner workflow.
        </p>
        <h2>Pricing posture: published flat vs a volume quote</h2>
        <p>
          Buildertrend pricing is custom as of 2026 — the public site collects builder type and
          construction volume, then a rep quotes you. Independent trackers still cite last-published
          entry tiers around $339–$499+ per month (unlimited users). Treat those as a baseline for
          the call, not a live rate card; details and sources live on
          <a href="/blog/buildertrend-pricing/">Buildertrend pricing and cost</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          BuildWorkPro publishes one number:
          <a href="/pricing/">$79/month</a> (or $790/year) with every feature and unlimited users, plus
          a 14-day trial with no credit card. There is no volume form and no demo gate. If you need the
          homeowner portal, that price does not buy it — and we will not pretend it does.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Product proof -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 text-center mb-4">
        Built around sub-to-GC billing
      </h2>
      <p class="text-center text-slate-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-10">
        Your contract value, change orders, schedule of values, and retainage stay connected from
        the first estimate to the final pay app.
      </p>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <div>
          <Screenshot
            src="/screenshots/12-pay-apps-list.png"
            alt="BuildWorkPro pay applications list showing billing periods, amounts, and approval status"
            caption="Every pay application across every project and GC, with status from draft to paid."
            width={1280}
            height={720}
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <Screenshot
            src="/screenshots/09-change-order-detail.png"
            alt="BuildWorkPro change order detail with scope, pricing, and approval workflow"
            caption="Change orders priced, submitted, and approved — your contract value stays current."
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <FAQSection faqs={faqs} />

    <!-- Related reading -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 text-center mb-8">
        Related comparisons and guides
      </h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-600">
        <li>
          <a href="/blog/buildertrend-pricing/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline font-medium"
            >Buildertrend pricing and cost</a
          >
          — last-published plans, quote posture, and switching costs (2026).
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="/blog/subcontractor-vs-general-contractor-software/"
            class="text-brand-600 hover:underline font-medium">Subcontractor vs GC software</a
          >
          — why the homeowner/builder stack is the wrong aisle for subs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="/compare/jobtread-alternative/"
            class="text-brand-600 hover:underline font-medium">BuildWorkPro vs JobTread</a
          >
          — the builder-first alternative that shows up next to Buildertrend most often.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="/compare/contractor-foreman-alternative/"
            class="text-brand-600 hover:underline font-medium">BuildWorkPro vs Contractor Foreman</a
          >
          — broader all-in-one with five tiers vs one flat sub plan.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/compare/knowify-alternative/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline font-medium"
            >BuildWorkPro vs Knowify</a
          >
          — the trade-focused option, with per-user pricing and job costing on its upper tier.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/compare/procore-alternative/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline font-medium"
            >BuildWorkPro vs Procore</a
          >
          — GC/owner platform vs the sub’s system of record.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline font-medium"
            >AIA-style pay applications</a
          >
          and
          <a
            href="/features/construction-bidding/"
            class="text-brand-600 hover:underline font-medium">construction bidding</a
          >
          — the two workflows this comparison turns on.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center">
      <div class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-900 px-8 py-14 sm:px-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight">
          Software that matches how subs actually get paid
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-slate-400 max-w-xl mx-auto">
          Every feature, unlimited users, $79/month flat. 14-day free trial — no credit card, no
          demo call.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-4">
          <a
            href={config.signupUrl}
            class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-brand-600 hover:bg-brand-700 text-white font-semibold text-sm transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-lg"
          >
            Start free trial
          </a>
          <a
            href="/contact/"
            class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-white/10 text-white font-semibold text-sm hover:bg-white/20 transition-colors"
          >
            Talk to us
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
          Comparing GC platforms too? See
          <a href="/compare/procore-alternative/" class="text-slate-300 underline hover:text-white"
            >BuildWorkPro vs Procore</a
          >. Other Buildertrend-adjacent options:
          <a href="/compare/jobtread-alternative/" class="text-slate-300 underline hover:text-white"
            >vs JobTread</a
          >
          and
          <a
            href="/compare/contractor-foreman-alternative/"
            class="text-slate-300 underline hover:text-white">vs Contractor Foreman</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
